Where supported by an upstream function's signature, an explicit
reflection mode can be selected with
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}as\_\allowbreak{}flags\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
or
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}as\_\allowbreak{}common\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}:

\begin{code}
constexpr auto ordinary_values = dsl::enum_values<Access, dsl::as_common<>>();
\end{code}

\section{Customization}\label{ch29-customization}

The default inclusive numeric search interval is
\texttt{[-128,\allowbreak{} 127]}. Change the global interval with
\texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}RANGE\_\allowbreak{}MIN}
and
\texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}RANGE\_\allowbreak{}MAX}
before including \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}, or specialize an
individual enum's range:

\begin{code}
enum class Opcode { Load = 1000, Store = 1010 };

template <> struct dsl::customize::enum_range<Opcode> {
  static constexpr int min = 1000;
  static constexpr int max = 1010;
};
\end{code}

\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}name\textless{}\allowbreak{}Value\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
does not scan the interval and can name enumerators outside it. Per-enum
search spans must remain smaller than \texttt{UINT16\_\allowbreak{}MAX}.

Name customization supports replacement names and exclusion:

\begin{code}
template <>
constexpr dsl::customize::customize_t
dsl::customize::enum_name<Opcode>(Opcode value) noexcept {
  switch (value) {
    case Opcode::Load: return "load";
    case Opcode::Store: return dsl::customize::invalid_tag;
    default: return dsl::customize::default_tag;
  }
}
\end{code}

\texttt{customize:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}enum\_\allowbreak{}type\_\allowbreak{}name\textless{}\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
customizes a type name in the same way.
\texttt{customize:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}enum\_\allowbreak{}range\textless{}\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}prefix\_\allowbreak{}length}
trims a prefix from discovered names.

Ranges, flag status, and prefix trimming can also be declared through
ADL in the enum's namespace:

\begin{code}
namespace lexer {
enum class Kind { TOK_Number = 1000, TOK_End = 1001 };

inline auto magic_enum_define_range_adl(Kind) {
  return dsl::customize::adl_info().minmax<1000, 1001>().prefix<4>();
}
}
static_assert(dsl::enum_name(lexer::Kind::TOK_Number) == "Number");
\end{code}

Declare all specializations and ADL hooks before the first reflection of
the affected enum. \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}customize} is
a namespace alias: use qualified specializations as above, or open
\texttt{namespace magic\_\allowbreak{}enum:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}customize}.

All upstream \texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}*}
configuration hooks are retained, including
\texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}CONFIG\_\allowbreak{}FILE},
custom optional/string/string-view aliases,
\texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}ENABLE\_\allowbreak{}HASH},
\texttt{MAGIC\_\allowbreak{}ENUM\_\allowbreak{}ENABLE\_\allowbreak{}HASH\_\allowbreak{}SWITCH},
assertion controls, and container exception controls. Configuration must
be consistent across translation units. The original per-header guards
also permit including external magic\_enum v0.9.8 headers before or
after \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}.

\section{Flags and operators}\label{ch29-flags-and-operators}

\begin{code}
enum class Access : unsigned { None = 0, Read = 1, Write = 2, Execute = 4 };
template <> struct dsl::customize::enum_range<Access> {
  static constexpr bool is_flags = true;
};
using namespace dsl::bitwise_operators;

auto permissions = Access::Read | Access::Write;
auto name = dsl::enum_flags_name(permissions); // "Read|Write"
auto parsed = dsl::enum_flags_cast<Access>("read,WRITE", ',', dsl::case_insensitive);
\end{code}

